一、8 分钟英文讲稿（已整合新版）

	你可以基本照读，个别地方我标了【可删】用来控时。

Slide 1 – Title & Context（≈30s）

	Hi everyone, today I’d like to share a new internal automation project I’ve been working on.

	The goal of this project is to automatically detect and process certain types of ServiceNow tickets, instead of relying on manual checking or ad-hoc scripts.

	I’ll walk you through the overall idea, the system design, and one real-world exception case we had to handle.

Slide 2 – Problem Statement（≈45s）

	The core problem we wanted to solve is fairly simple:

	We have users creating ServiceNow tickets for operational requests, for example, copying data in MinIO.

	Traditionally, someone had to periodically check for new tickets, validate them, and then manually trigger the corresponding actions.

	This is slow, error-prone, and doesn’t scale well when the number of requests increases.

	So the idea is: once a request is approved, we automatically detect it, route it to the right handler, and start a controlled execution flow with full auditability.

Slide 3 – High-Level Architecture（≈1 min）

	At a high level, this project is a polling-based automation service.

	We periodically poll the ServiceNow API to check if there are any newly created or updated tickets that match certain criteria.

	The polling itself is implemented using Python’s scheduling mechanism, so we can easily configure how often we want to run it — for example, every 5 minutes or every 10 minutes.

	Once we fetch the tickets, we don’t process them directly.

	Instead, we route them through a configuration-driven pipeline, which I’ll explain next.

Slide 4 – Configuration-Driven Routing（≈1.5 min）

	One of the key design ideas is that the whole routing logic is configuration-based.

	We use a YAML file to define different handlers.

	Each handler section specifies a set of conditions.

	These conditions are used to query the ServiceNow API — for example, by request type, category, or some custom fields.

	If a ticket matches a certain condition block, it will be routed to the corresponding handler.

	This makes the system highly extensible.

	If we introduce a new type of request in the future, we don’t need to rewrite the core logic.

	We just add a new section in the YAML file with a new set of filters and a new handler name.

	The core engine stays the same, and the new flow follows the same pattern.

Slide 5 – Validators & Notifiers（≈1 min）

	On top of handlers, we also introduced two pluggable components: validators and notifiers.

	Validators run before a handler is executed.

	They are used to make sure the ticket is actually ready to be processed.

	For example, we have a validator that checks whether the user’s manager has approved the request.

	Notifiers are used to send feedback after or during processing.

	Right now, our default notifiers include:
		•	Sending an email
	•	Posting a comment back to the ServiceNow ticket

	Both validators and notifiers are optional and configurable, so different request types can have different pre-checks and notification strategies.

Slide 6 – Two-Stage Processing & Dry-Run Mode（≈1.5 min）

	For the MinIO copy request specifically, we use a two-stage processing design.

	In the first stage, the ServiceNow handler does not directly execute the copy operation.

	Instead, it extracts structured information from the ticket description — for example, source path, destination path, and requester details.

	It then inserts these fields into a separate request table.

	There is another worker program that continuously scans this table and performs the actual copy operation.

	Right now, this whole flow is running in a dry-run mode.

	That means when we insert a record into the request table, we set an is_active = false flag.

	So the worker program will not pick it up automatically.

	For now, we manually flip is_active to true when we want to trigger the real execution.

	This gives us a safe rollout mechanism and allows us to validate the end-to-end logic before enabling full automation.

Slide 7 – End-to-End Flow（≈1 min）

	Putting everything together, the end-to-end flow looks like this:

	A user submits a ServiceNow ticket, for example a MinIO copy request.

	After the request is approved by the user’s manager, our service detects it during the next polling cycle.

	The ticket is routed to the correct handler based on the YAML configuration.

	All configured validators are run.

	If validation passes, the handler extracts the request details and inserts a record into the request table.

	The worker program scans the table and, when is_active is true, performs the actual copy operation.

	The execution result is stored in a result table.

	Finally, the whole process is recorded in an audit table and notifications are sent back to the user.

Slide 8 – Async Checker & Result Feedback（≈1 min）

	To improve user feedback, we also added an asynchronous checker.

	After the handler inserts a record into the request table, it schedules a background check a few minutes later.

	This checker queries the result table, which is populated by the worker program after execution.

	If a result is found, the checker posts the execution status or summary back to the original ServiceNow ticket as a comment.

	This feature is only enabled when dry-run mode is turned off.

	In the current dry-run setup, this checker is disabled because there is no real execution result yet.

	But once we move to full automation, this gives users near real-time feedback without manual intervention.

Slide 9 – Real-World Exception Case（≈1.5 min）

	In theory, the flow could be even simpler.

	Ideally, we would just query ServiceNow for tickets whose state is “Approved” and process them directly.

	But in practice, we ran into a real-world constraint.

	For this MinIO copy request, there is an additional approval step by a specific group.

	However, that group only has two approvers, and they are both in the US.

	So for users in China or India time zones, it’s not realistic to block their requests until that second approval happens.

	As a workaround, we decided that once the user’s direct manager approves the request, we will go ahead and process it.

	This means we can’t rely purely on the ticket state anymore.

	So we added a custom validator.

	This validator checks whether the user’s manager has approved the request.

	If yes, we parse the JSON payload from the ticket description and continue processing.

	If not, we mark the ticket as “validation failed,” but we don’t discard it.

	It will be retried in the next polling cycle.

	This is a temporary workaround, but it allows us to keep the user experience smooth without breaking the overall design.

Slide 10 – Configuration Center (Popstore)（≈1 min）

	Another part of the design is the configuration center.

	All YAML configurations are stored in Popstore.

	When the service starts up, it fetches the latest configuration via a RESTful API.

	This allows us to update routing rules, validators, or notifier settings without redeploying the service.

	From an operational point of view, this gives us much more flexibility and reduces the risk of hotfix deployments.

Slide 11 – Summary & Q&A（≈45s）

	To summarize:

	This project is a polling-based ServiceNow automation service.

	It uses a configuration-driven routing design, so new request types can be added with minimal code changes.

	For the MinIO copy request, we use a two-stage processing model with a safe dry-run mode.

	We also added an asynchronous checker to feed execution results back to ServiceNow.

	The system supports pluggable validators and notifiers.

	And it includes a pragmatic workaround to handle real-world approval constraints.

	That’s pretty much what I wanted to share today.

	I’m happy to take any questions.

